154 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (Abstract This research paper examines the efficacy of Convolutional Neural Networks \(CNNs\) in recognizing facial emotions across seven distinct categories: anger, disgust, fear, happiness, neutral, sadness, and surprise. Using a dataset of 7,178 facial images, we implemented a CNN model that achieved an overall validation accuracy of 54.45%, with significant variation in classification performance across different emotion categories. Happiness and surprise were recognized with relatively high reliability \(F1-scores of 0.74 and 0.70 respectively\), while emotions such as fear and sadness presented greater classification challenges \(F1-scores of 0.38 and 0.40\). This paper presents a comprehensive analysis of the model's performance, identifies patterns in misclassification through confusion matrix analysis, and proposes potential enhancements to improve emotion recognition accuracy.)
/E (Abstract This research paper examines the efficacy of Convolutional Neural Networks \(CNNs\) in recognizing facial emotions across seven distinct categories: anger, disgust, fear, happiness, neutral, sadness, and surprise. Using a dataset of 7,178 facial images, we implemented a CNN model that achieved an overall validation accuracy of 54.45%, with significant variation in classification performance across different emotion categories. Happiness and surprise were recognized with relatively high reliability \(F1-scores of 0.74 and 0.70 respectively\), while emotions such as fear and sadness presented greater classification challenges \(F1-scores of 0.38 and 0.40\). This paper presents a comprehensive analysis of the model's performance, identifies patterns in misclassification through confusion matrix analysis, and proposes potential enhancements to improve emotion recognition accuracy.)
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
